### Phase 2 — Prove the dev/community slice end-to-end
Run on **dev / pass-community only** first. This validates every in-scope
assumption with reversible changes and **no IAM edits**.

**Step A — Recon (read-only), as `dbcommunity`:**
```bash
# Terminal 1
get-to-rds -d community
# Terminal 2
get-to-psql -d community
```
```sql
SELECT current_user, current_database();
SELECT tableowner, count(*) FROM pg_tables WHERE schemaname='public' GROUP BY tableowner;
\dn
```
Confirm the table owner; adjust `\set owner` if needed. `\q` to exit.

**Step B — Create roles, as the MASTER user:**
```bash
# Get master creds (Contributor can read this secret)
SECRET_ARN=$(aws rds describe-db-clusters --db-cluster-identifier pass-community \
  --query 'DBClusters[0].MasterUserSecret.SecretArn' --output text \
  --region us-east-2 --profile pass-dev-contributor)

aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id "$SECRET_ARN" \
  --query SecretString --output text --region us-east-2 --profile pass-dev-contributor
# -> {"username":"dbadmin","password":"..."}

# Run the script through the existing tunnel (Terminal 1 must still be open)
psql "host=localhost port=15433 dbname=community user=dbadmin \
  sslmode=require sslrootcert=system gssencmode=disable" \
  -f aws-access-tools/db-roles/core-1745-roles.sql
```

**Step C — Validate WRITE path (Contributor → `app_rw` IAM token):**
```bash
HOST=$(aws rds describe-db-clusters --db-cluster-identifier pass-community \
  --query 'DBClusters[0].Endpoint' --output text --region us-east-2 --profile pass-dev-contributor)

TOKEN=$(aws rds generate-db-auth-token --hostname "$HOST" --port 5432 \
  --username app_rw --region us-east-2 --profile pass-dev-contributor)

PGPASSWORD="$TOKEN" psql "host=localhost port=15433 dbname=community user=app_rw \
  sslmode=require sslrootcert=system gssencmode=disable"
```
```sql
SELECT current_user;                                       -- expect app_rw
CREATE TEMP TABLE _t(x int); INSERT INTO _t VALUES (1);    -- write succeeds
```

**Step D — Validate READ path is genuinely read-only (`app_ro`):**
```bash
TOKEN=$(aws rds generate-db-auth-token --hostname "$HOST" --port 5432 \
  --username app_ro --region us-east-2 --profile pass-dev-contributor)
PGPASSWORD="$TOKEN" psql "host=localhost port=15433 dbname=community user=app_ro \
  sslmode=require sslrootcert=system gssencmode=disable"
```
```sql
SELECT current_user;            -- expect app_ro
SELECT 1;                       -- read works
CREATE TEMP TABLE _t2(x int);   -- expect: ERROR permission denied
```
**Slice passes** when write works as `app_rw` and is denied as `app_ro`.
This already satisfies both acceptance criteria.

