## 10. Proposed `db_user()` change (Phase 3)
Current `db_user()` resolves db name → master/service account. Change it to take
the access level so the username follows the tier (`app_ro`/`app_rw` are identical
across clusters; the *database* still selects host/port).

```bash
# configs/common/root.profile
# Usage: user=$(db_user "<db_name>" "<access_level>")
db_user() {
  local db_name="$1"
  local access="$2"
  case "$access" in
    r) echo "app_ro" ;;                       # read tier
    c) echo "app_rw" ;;                        # read-write tier
    o)                                         # owner = admin / break-glass
      case "$db_name" in
        admin)        echo "${RDS_USER}" ;;
        community)    echo "dbcommunity" ;;
        eligibility)  echo "dbeligibility" ;;
        offerings)    echo "dbofferings" ;;
        *)            echo "dbadmin" ;;
      esac ;;
    *) echo "dbadmin" ;;
  esac
}
```
Update callers in `get-to-psql` and `get-to-rds` to pass `${access_level}` as the
second arg: `rds_user="$(db_user "${db_name}" "${access_level}")"`.

